# How does a CI Pipeline work?
⭐ Interview Answer

“When a developer pushes code to Git, it triggers the CI pipeline. The pipeline first checks out the latest source code. Then it builds the application, runs automated tests, performs code-quality and security checks, and finally creates a versioned artifact.

If any stage fails, the pipeline stops and the team gets feedback. Only code that passes all required checks moves forward.”


# What is an Artifact and why is it important?
⭐ Interview Answer
“An artifact is the output produced by the build process that can be deployed to an environment.

For example, in a Java application it could be a JAR file, and in a containerized application it could be a Docker image.

Artifacts are important because they are versioned, traceable, and can be promoted across environments without rebuilding the application.”

# How does CD Pipeline work?
⭐ Interview Answer

“After CI successfully creates the artifact, CD takes that artifact and handles the deployment process.

Usually, the artifact is deployed first to Dev. After validation, it is promoted to QA, then UAT, and finally Production. Depending on the organization, some environments may require manual approval before promotion.”

# Why deploy the same artifact across Dev, QA, UAT & Production?
⭐ Interview Answer
“We deploy the same artifact across all environments to ensure consistency.

If we rebuild the application separately for Dev, QA, UAT, and Production, there is a possibility that the builds can be different.

Instead, we build the application once, test that artifact, and promote the exact same artifact through all environments.”

# What are Environment-Specific Configurations?

This is where you can show real DevOps knowledge.
⭐ Interview Answer
“The application code and artifact should remain the same, but some configuration values are different for each environment.

For example, Dev, QA, and Production may use different database endpoints, API URLs, credentials, replica counts, or feature configurations.

So we keep these values outside the application artifact and inject the appropriate configuration during deployment.”

# What happens when deployment fails?
⭐ Interview Answer

“If a deployment fails, we don't continue promoting that version to the next environment.

First, I would check the pipeline logs, deployment events, application logs, and health checks to identify the root cause.

If the issue can be fixed quickly, I would fix it and redeploy. If the new version is already causing problems in Production, I would rollback to the previous stable version.”

# What is Rollback in CI/CD?
⭐ Interview Answer

“Rollback means moving the application back to the last known stable version when the current deployment has a problem.

For example, if version 2.0 is deployed to Production and starts causing errors, we can rollback to version 1.9 while we investigate the issue.”



